{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"In the current public SCOPe base release 2.08 (latest periodic-update label 2.08-2026-08-16), these are distinct families within the same NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold superfamily: alcohol dehydrogenase-like C-terminal domains are c.2.1.1/SUNID 51736, CoA-binding domains are c.2.1.8/SUNID 51900, and potassium-channel NAD-binding domains are c.2.1.9/SUNID 63944. Their common parent is superfamily c.2.1/SUNID 51735, within fold c.2/SUNID 51734 and class c (alpha/beta proteins)/SUNID 51349.

Thus SCOPe is proposing domain-level remote homology—a probable common ancestor despite potentially weak sequence similarity—not merely a coincidentally similar fold. The API explicitly marks homology as implied at the c.2.1 superfamily level and reports no curated homology warnings or heterogeneity for these nodes. However, separate family placement records substantial divergence and is stronger evidence against transferring detailed function than their shared superfamily is evidence for it. The family names themselves are not universal functional annotations: one must not infer alcohol-dehydrogenase chemistry or substrate specificity for CoA-binding or potassium-channel domains, infer CoA specificity for the other families, or transfer channel/gating function to either soluble family. Homology applies to the classified domain, not automatically to whole multidomain proteins, complexes, oligomeric states, or biological roles.

A same-superfamily structure can be a remote fold or domain-boundary template, but functional-site or ligand modeling should preferentially use a same-family template with adequate aligned coverage, conserved binding/catalytic residues, compatible ligand state, and matching domain context. No single c.2.1 representative safely represents all three families; representatives should be selected separately per family and for the intended property. Function assignments need evidence beyond SCOPe—family-sensitive sequence/profile and residue analysis, structural comparison, and direct ligand-binding or enzymatic evidence; any proposed potassium-channel role additionally requires appropriate channel-context and electrophysiological evidence.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/","/api/v1/openapi.yaml","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=alcohol-dehydrogenase-like","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=alcohol%20dehydrogenase","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=CoA-binding","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=potassium-channel%20NAD-binding","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=Rossmann&mode=token&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=CoA&mode=token&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=potassium&mode=token&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/children?limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51736/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51900/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/63944/homology"]}]}
